---
import Entry from '../../layouts/Entry.astro';
import Note from '../../components/Note.astro';
import Plate from '../../components/Plate.astro';
import Specimen from '../../components/Specimen.astro';
---
<Entry
  slug="nfl-overtime-4th-down"
  image="/img/fg-explainer.png"
  links={[
    { href: 'https://playbyplay.football', label: 'Try the live tool' },
    { href: 'https://github.com/EshaanDhavala/nfl-ot-4th-down-model', label: 'Model code' },
  ]}
>
  <h2><span class="label">The question</span>4th down in overtime: go, punt, or kick?</h2>
  <p>
    NFL overtime changed in 2022 and again in 2024, and the old 4th-down charts don’t account for it. If
    you score first, the other team still gets the ball. So the “right” call depends on which possession
    you’re in, what the other team just did, and how much you trust your kicker in this weather.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our Bruin Sports Analytics team built <a href="https://playbyplay.football">playbyplay.football</a>,
    a decision engine that takes the full game situation and returns a win probability for each choice. It
    runs four XGBoost sub-models combined by expected value.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="label">My part</span>The field-goal make-probability model</h2>
  <Note>Blocked kicks are excluded on purpose. A block is a defensive event, not a miss by the kicker.</Note>
  <p>
    Every unblocked NFL field goal from <mark>2016–2024</mark>, joined with game weather, stadium, and each
    kicker’s history. The model sees 15 features, including:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The kick:</strong> distance, surface, dome vs. outdoors, altitude (yes, Denver matters).</li>
    <li><strong>The weather:</strong> wind gusts and temperature (each also crossed with distance, since both matter more on long kicks), and precipitation.</li>
    <li><strong>The kicker:</strong> rolling 6-game make rate and career make rate at that distance, plus how many attempts that career rate is based on.</li>
    <li><strong>The pressure:</strong> time left, score differential, overtime or not.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It’s gradient-boosted trees tuned by randomized search on <em>Brier score</em>, then isotonic-calibrated. A
    “70%” really needs to mean 70%, because the engine multiplies it against other probabilities. The
    train/test split is stratified by season so every era of kicking is in both sets.
  </p>

  <Plate src="/img/fg-explainer.png" alt="Four-panel chart: make probability falls with distance, elite kickers hold up better at 45–52 yards, weather has modest effects, and scenario predictions range from 47% to 100%" fig="1" wide>
    The one-slide version I presented. Distance dominates, but an elite kicker is worth 12 points over an average one at
    45 yards.
  </Plate>

  <Plate src="/img/fg-heatmap.png" alt="Heatmap of make probability by kick distance and wind gust speed, with contour lines at 95, 90, 80 and 65 percent" fig="2" tilt={-0.6}>
    Distance × wind gust for a league-average kicker. Strong gusts pull the 95% line in by about 3–4 yards.
  </Plate>

  <Specimen rows={[
    ['Kicks modeled', 'all unblocked FGs, 2016–24'],
    ['Features', '15'],
    ['Model', 'XGBoost + calibration'],
    ['League make rate', '85.8%'],
  ]} />
</Entry>
